## About this package

This package is part of the ${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MAJOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MINOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_PATCH} release of Emgu LiteRT. It contains:

- `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Maui.dll`: `ChatPage`, a whole chat page for an app - the model picker, saved chats, memories, a settings sheet, and an agent whose skills an app adds or removes - and `ChatView`, the MAUI chat control it is built on, for the on-device `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MODELS_NUGET_ID}` language models, or any `IChatClient`. The view shows the user's messages and the replies - streamed as they are written, with their thinking, tool steps, Markdown, code blocks with Copy, and maths - a message box with Send and Stop, an attach button for images and audio, a microphone for voice messages, and for an on-device model a card that downloads and loads it. Replies can be read aloud.

The conversation logic comes from `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_CHAT_NUGET_ID}` (`ChatConversation`), the agent's skills from `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_AGENTS_NUGET_ID}` (`ChatSkill`, `ChatSkillSet`). The package has no native runtime dependency: add the LiteRT-LM native runtime packages for your platforms, as for `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MODELS_NUGET_ID}`.

`ChatPage` and the skill API are new in this release and may still change.

## A chat page in a few lines

```csharp
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Chat;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Chat.Sqlite;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Maui;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Models;

var store = new ChatHistoryStore(Path.Combine(FileSystem.AppDataDirectory, "MyApp", "chats"));
var options = new ChatPageOptions { Title = "Assistant", History = store };
options.Models.Add(new ChatModelOption("Gemma4E2B", "Gemma 4 E2B", () => new Gemma4E2B())
    { DownloadSize = 2600L << 20, CanUseTools = true, AcceptsImages = true, AcceptsAudio = true, CanSummarize = true });
foreach (ChatFeature feature in ChatPage.DefaultFeatures(new SqliteAgentMemoryStore(store), new SqliteAgentGoalStore(store)))
    options.Features.Add(feature);
MainPage = new ChatPage(options);          // in App, or push it on a NavigationPage
```

and in MauiProgram, `builder.UseMauiApp<App>().UseLiteRtChat()`. That gives a page with a header (the model, Chats, New, Settings), the chat, chips above the message box (Think, Web search), and the settings - a bottom sheet, or a side panel on screens 820 px and wider - with the page's own settings (read aloud, private chat, developer mode) and each feature's section. `ChatPageOptions` also takes the instructions (`Instructions` for a model with tools, `InstructionsWithoutTools` for one without, `PrivateInstructions`), where settings are kept (`Settings`: MAUI's Preferences with the `litert_lm_chat_` prefix by default), `ConfigureClient` to wrap the model's client (logging, a server), and `AttachmentSource` / `ReplySpeaker` as the view's. `ChatModelOption` says what each model can do (`CanUseTools`, `AcceptsImages`, `AcceptsAudio`, `CanThink`, `CanSummarize`, `ContextWindow` and `MaxContextWindow` - with a maximum, the settings offer the window size -, `Backends` - "cpu" and "gpu" -, `ModelFile` for the list to say what is downloaded); a remote model (a server's) is an option without a factory, reached through its `Remote` (an `IRemoteChatModel`, as `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MAUI_OPENAI_NUGET_ID}`'s `ServerFeature` is) or the client a derived page makes (`CreateChatClient`, `ConnectRemoteAsync`). Saved chats need `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_CHAT_SQLITE_NUGET_ID}`'s `ChatHistoryStore` (an `IChatHistoryStore`), memories and goals its `SqliteAgentMemoryStore` and `SqliteAgentGoalStore`; the files a feature keeps with a reply (a document it wrote, a picture it drew: `IChatPageHost.AddCardToReply(card, file)`) are saved with the chat and shown again when it is opened (`ChatFeature.RestoreSavedFile`); `AutoSave` off lets an app save the chats itself, and `ChatActions` adds buttons to each chat's row. The page downloads a model with `BackgroundDownloads` (the download goes on in the background, a paused one continues - the card says "Continue" - and "Not now" puts it off), offers the CPU when a GPU load fails, and reloads the same model for another backend, context window or thread count with the chat going on (`ReloadModelAsync`; "Reading the conversation so far..." with the next message). `ShowCpuThreads` adds the thread slider, `ShowContextWindow` the window choice and the "Conversation memory" usage with "Compact now", `ShowBackButton` a back button. The page's members: `Chat` (the view), `Conversation`, `SelectedModel` / `SelectModel` / `ReloadModelAsync`, `CurrentChatId` / `OpenChatAsync` / `StartNewChat`, `IsPrivate` / `SetPrivateAsync`, `HandsFree` / `SetHandsFree`, `Skills`, `Features`, `SetStatus`, `AskModelOnceAsync`, `ExchangeCompleted`; for a derived page, the virtual `SendCoreAsync`, `ComposeOptionsAsync`, `OnBeforeStreamingAsync`, `ConfigureConversation`, `CreateChatClient`, `ConnectRemoteAsync`, `OpenChatAsync`, `StartNewChat`, `OnPrivateModeChangingAsync` and `OnPageBuilt`.

### Skills and features

The agent's tools come from the page's features. A `ChatSkill` (`${EMGU_LITERT_LM_AGENTS_NUGET_ID}`) is the pure part: the tools it adds with every message (`Tools`), tool groups the model gets only when a message needs them (`ToolGroups`, routed by `ToolRouter`), its instructions (`InstructionsAsync`, and `DisabledInstructions` while it is off), `Enabled`, and what happens on a new conversation. A `ChatFeature` is a skill with the page's UI: a settings section (`BuildSettingsSection`, with `ChatPage.SwitchRow`, `SectionTitle`, `Note`, `Link`), a chip above the message box (`BuildComposerChip`), cards on replies, its own step lines (`DescribeToolCall`, `OnToolStepCompleted`) and the page's life (`OnAttached`, `OnAppearing`, `OnModelChanged`, `OnReplyCompleted`, `OnChatOpened`...), reaching the page through `Host` (`IChatPageHost`: the conversation, the model, the status line, alerts, prompts, the bottom sheet, and `Overlays` - views over the chat area, such as a camera preview, or over the whole page). `SkillFeature<TSkill>` puts UI on an existing skill. A feature's `Enabled` is kept in the settings (`feature_{Name}`) unless `PersistEnabled` says otherwise. The whole of a skill with one tool:

```csharp
public sealed class DiceFeature : ChatFeature
{
    public override string Name => "dice";
    public override IEnumerable<AITool> Tools(ChatMessageContext context) => new AITool[]
    {
        AIFunctionFactory.Create((int sides) => Random.Shared.Next(1, sides + 1), "roll_die", "Rolls a die with the given number of sides.")
    };
    public override View BuildSettingsSection() => ChatPage.SwitchRow("Dice", "Lets the assistant roll dice.", Enabled, on => Enabled = on);
}
```

The built-in features (`ChatPage.DefaultFeatures`, each one a class in `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Maui.Features` an app can leave out; two more are added by hand, with the `ChatHistoryStore` and a `SearchServices` - `new SearchServices(new EmbeddingModelChoice(() => new EmbeddingGemma2TextVision(), "EmbeddingGemma 2"))` - they share: `PastChatsFeature`, which searches the saved chats with the embedding model, and `PhotoSearchFeature`, which indexes the device's most recent photos with it and shows the ones the user asks for; and, without the search model, `WrittenDocumentsFeature` (the create_document tool: a Word, PDF or Markdown file from the model's Markdown, with a card to open or share it) and `MeetingFeature` (record_meeting: a meeting written down by the platform's speech recognizer as it goes, then its notes by the model; Android 13+, iOS and Mac Catalyst, with the microphone and speech permissions) - places come from an embedded list of towns from GeoNames (CC BY 4.0, https://www.geonames.org/), and the app needs photo access: `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription` in Info.plist on iOS and Mac Catalyst, the permissions the library declares on Android): `CoreToolsFeature` (the date and time, the calculator, unit conversion), `MemoryFeature` (memories about the user, with the "Remember things about me" switch and the list), `WebFeature` (the weather, Wikipedia, currency conversion, reading pages; web search with a Tavily API key entered in the settings and kept in secure storage; the "Web search" chip), `LocationFeature` (the user's town, off until "Share my location" is on; the app declares `ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION` / `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`), `ClipboardFeature` (only for a message about what the user copied) and `GoalsFeature` (the user's goals and their progress); `ExportFeature` (added by hand with an exporter, e.g. `new ExportFeature((id, stream, cancel) => ChatExporter.ExportAsync(store, id, stream, cancel))` over the `ChatHistoryStore`: a saved chat as a ZIP with its Markdown transcript, pictures, recordings and attached document, shared through the platform's share sheet from a button on each chat's row and in the settings); `StorageFeature` (added by hand with a `StorageCatalog` over the `ChatHistoryStore` - the app's models, its own folders, the chats' documents, indexes, attachments, database and exports - and the `SearchServices`: a page that lists them with their sizes and the free space to delete them, releasing the model in use first and stopping the features' work on them through `IStorageParticipant`; a deleted search model turns the searches off until it is downloaded again from the page or the notices under their switches); `DeviceActionsFeature` (timers, alarms, calendar events, reminders and directions, the ones the platform can do: the model's tool asks, a card above the reply shows what will be done with Confirm and Cancel, and only a confirmed action is done - Android's clock, calendar and maps apps, EventKit on iOS and Mac Catalyst with `NSCalendarsWriteOnlyAccessUsageDescription` and `NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription` in Info.plist, Bing Maps on Windows); `TaskFeature` (the "Plan" chip: the message is first broken into a short plan the user can edit, skip steps of and stop, each step then runs as a request of its own with its tool calls as step lines, and the answer is written from the results; a feature's `CreateMessageClient` is how a message runs through a client of its own); `VoiceLanguageFeature` (the language voice messages are recognized in, for the speech recognizer that writes them down - the device's unless chosen); `VoiceModeFeature` (the "Voice chat" chip: a hands-free conversation - it listens, sends what was said once the speaker pauses, has the reply read aloud as it is written and listens again; a tap, or speech with headphones, interrupts; the app needs the microphone permission, and the speech recognition one for a model without audio). The sample app `Emgu.LiteRT.Example/MauiChat` in the source repository is a `ChatPage` with them and a `DiceFeature`.

More features come in packages of their own, so this one stays small: `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MAUI_DOCUMENTS_NUGET_ID}` (`DocumentsFeature`: a PDF, Word or text file attached to the chat and asked about), `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MAUI_MCP_NUGET_ID}` (`McpFeature`: Model Context Protocol tool servers the user adds in the settings), `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MAUI_OPENAI_NUGET_ID}` (`ServerFeature`: a model on a server of the user's own - LM Studio, Ollama or any OpenAI-compatible API - as the chat's remote model option; any other remote model is an `IRemoteChatModel` on a `ChatModelOption.Remote`), `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MAUI_IMAGES_NUGET_ID}` (`ImageFeature`: pictures the model draws with Z-Image-Turbo on the device) and `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MAUI_CV_NUGET_ID}` (`CameraFeature` and `ExerciseFeature`: the live camera, camera and sound monitors with alerts, photos and clips, and counting exercises, with Emgu CV).

## ChatView on its own

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:chat="clr-namespace:Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Maui;assembly=Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Maui"
             x:Class="MyApp.MainPage">
    <chat:ChatView x:Name="Chat" Instructions="You are a helpful assistant. Keep your answers short." />
</ContentPage>
```

```csharp
public MainPage()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    Chat.Model = new Qwen3();   // downloaded (after a tap on the card) and loaded by the view
}
```

That is a whole chat, without the page around it. Instead of `Model`, give the view a `ChatClient` (a server, another provider, or a client with tools - with `Model` too if it runs that model), or a `Conversation` of your own, e.g. one opened from a saved chat with `ChatConversation.Load`. Other properties: `ThinkingEnabled`, `Options` (copied for each message: tools, the longest reply...) or `OptionsProvider` (makes each message's options as it is sent, for an agent whose tools depend on the message) with `RerunRequested` (asked after each reply whether to send the message again into the same reply, for a tool group that ended the request), `LoadStateCreated` (to set a new model's backend, name or engine settings before it loads), `Placeholder`, `AttachmentKinds` / `ShowAttachButton` / `AttachmentSource` (by default `MediaChatAttachmentSource`: the photo library, the camera or an audio file; images are scaled to 1024 px JPEG by `ChatImages.PrepareAsync`; taking photos needs the camera permission in the app's manifest / Info.plist), `ReadAloud` / `ReplySpeaker` (by default `TextToSpeechReplySpeaker`, sentence by sentence while the reply streams), `Header` (a view above the transcript) and `Toolbar` (a view above the message box, e.g. chips). `LoadState` (a `ModelLoadState`) tells how loading the model goes, `ExchangeCompleted` is raised after each reply (save it then) and `ModelLoadFailed` when the model can't be loaded. `SendAsync(text, attachments, context)` sends a message from code. The message box is a `ChatPromptEditor`: with a keyboard, Enter sends and Shift+Enter adds a line (`UseLiteRtChat()` registers its Mac Catalyst handler).

### Saving chats

`${EMGU_LITERT_LM_CHAT_SQLITE_NUGET_ID}` keeps chats in a SQLite database. Attach it to the view's conversation to save every exchange, and load a saved chat into it to continue:

```csharp
var store = new ChatHistoryStore(Path.Combine(FileSystem.AppDataDirectory, "MyApp", "chats"));
ChatHistory history = await store.LoadAsync(lastChatId);    // null if there is none
history?.Load(Chat.Conversation);
ChatHistoryBinding saving = store.Attach(Chat.Conversation, history != null ? lastChatId : 0, "Qwen3");
saving.ChatCreated += (s, e) => Preferences.Default.Set("last_chat", saving.ChatId);
```

Give the store a folder named after the app: on Mac Catalyst, `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory` can be `~/Library` itself, which other apps use too.

### Voice messages

The microphone records a voice message (tap, speak, tap again to send; at most `MaxVoiceLength`, 30 s). With `VoiceMessages = Auto` (the default) a model that takes audio, such as Gemma 4, gets the recording, and the words are written down under it (`ChatMessageItem.Transcript`, never given to the model; `TranscribeVoiceMessages`). Any other model or client gets the words the platform's speech recognizer writes down, where it can: Android 13 and later, iOS and Mac Catalyst. Elsewhere, such as on Android 12 with a text-only model, there is no microphone. `Audio`, `Text` and `Off` choose it yourself, and `EffectiveVoiceMessages` tells what it is now.

The app declares the permissions:

- Android: `android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO`, and a `<queries>` entry for the `android.speech.RecognitionService` intent;
- iOS and Mac Catalyst: `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` and `NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription` in Info.plist;
- Windows: the `microphone` capability. Windows has no speech recognizer here, so only models that take audio get a microphone.

The parts are public for an app's own UI: `VoiceRecorder` (16 kHz mono WAV, with microphone levels for a voice chat), `SpeechRecognizer` (`TranscribeAsync`; `Language` - one of `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Chat`'s `VoiceLanguages`, or the device's) and `MicrophoneGate`, which an always-on listener such as a sound monitor uses to let go of the microphone while a recording needs it. On Android the on-device recognizer is used when it has the language; otherwise the normal one, which may send the audio to Google's service.

`MicrophoneSoundSource` delivers the microphone as 16 kHz samples while they arrive, for listeners that run for a long time (a sound monitor, meeting notes); it is an `ISoundSource` of `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_AGENTS_NUGET_ID}`, whose `ClipSoundSource` plays a clip through the same path (Android, iOS and Mac Catalyst; `IsSupported`). `TextLanguage.DetectAsync` gives a text's language - its writing system, and for Latin script Apple's recognizer on iOS and Mac Catalyst, or your own identifier (`TextLanguage.IdentifyLatin`, e.g. ML Kit on Android) - and `TextToSpeechReplySpeaker` reads replies with a voice for it. `AudioFilePlayer.PlayAsync` plays a sound file once or repeated for a number of seconds; `InlineVideoView` (Android) plays a video in place, after `builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(h => h.AddHandler<InlineVideoView, InlineVideoHandler>())`.

`PdfPageRenderer` renders a PDF's pages to JPEG with each platform's own renderer (Android's PdfRenderer, PDFKit on iOS and Mac Catalyst, Windows.Data.Pdf), for pages that are images - scans, charts drawn as shapes. It is the `IPdfPageRenderer` that `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_AGENTS_DOCUMENTS_NUGET_ID}`' `DocumentReader` takes (`OpenPdf = pdf => new PdfPageRenderer(pdf)`).

For models on a phone:

- `BackgroundDownloads.DownloadFileAsync` downloads a model's file and keeps the download going when the app leaves the screen, as far as each platform allows: a foreground service with a progress notification and a Pause button on Android (this package declares it, with the permissions it needs, in the app's manifest), a background task on iOS (which iOS ends after a short time; the download is then paused) and Mac Catalyst. A paused download continues from what it received. `BackgroundDownloads.Hint` says what to tell people on each platform.
- `DeviceWorkload.ReadAsync` reads the battery and the heat for `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_AGENTS_NUGET_ID}`' `WorkloadPolicy`, which says when heavy work should wait: `await new WorkloadPolicy().WaitAsync(DeviceWorkload.ReadAsync, () => false, reason => ..., token)`.
- `CpuThreadSettings.Count` is one CPU thread count for all of an app's models, kept in its preferences (at most the number of cores, 4 by default).

## Changing the look

The controls are templated. Each finds its template, and the styles of its buttons, by key - first in the app's resources, then in the package's own `ChatStyles` - so an app replaces one by defining a resource with its key in `App.xaml`; nothing needs to be merged. The colours and fonts are keys of the package's `ChatTheme` (merged into the app's resources under the app's own, the first time a control needs them), so `<Color x:Key="ChatAccent">#1E9E6A</Color>` in `App.xaml` recolours every control, and `<x:String x:Key="ChatBodyFont">MyFont</x:String>` sets their font (a family registered with `ConfigureFonts`):

| Key | What it is |
|---|---|
| `ChatAccent`, `ChatDanger`, `ChatSuccess`, `ChatPrimaryText{Light,Dark}`, `ChatSecondaryText{Light,Dark}`, `ChatReplyBubble{Light,Dark}`, `ChatSurface{Light,Dark}`, `ChatPageBackground{Light,Dark}`, `ChatRowBorder{Light,Dark}`, `ChatSelectedRow{Light,Dark}`, `ChatCodeBackground`, `ChatCodeText`, `ChatCodeSecondaryText` | The colours (`ChatTheme`); the pairs follow the app's light or dark theme |
| `ChatTitleFont`, `ChatBodyFont`, `ChatMonoFont` | The fonts, as `x:String` (empty for the platform's default) |
| `ChatPageTemplate` | The page: the header's `PART_Title`, `PART_Model`, `PART_Chats`, `PART_NewChat`, `PART_Settings`; `PART_Chat` (the `ChatView`), `PART_Status`, `PART_SidePanel` / `PART_SideContent` (wide screens), `PART_Sheet` (a `BottomSheet`) |
| `ChatHeaderButtonStyle`, `ChatModelButtonStyle`, `ChatChipStyle`, `ChatChipOnStyle` | The page's header buttons and model button; a `ToggleChip` off and on |
| `ChatSheetStyle`, `ChatSheetTitleStyle`, `ChatSheetCloseStyle` | A `BottomSheet`'s card, title and closing button |
| `ChatSectionTitleStyle`, `ChatSectionTextStyle`, `ChatSectionNoteStyle`, `ChatLinkStyle`, `ChatDividerStyle` | Settings sections: a heading, a row's text, a note, a tappable line, the line between sections |
| `ChatListTitleStyle`, `ChatListDetailStyle`, `ChatListRowStyle` | A list row (saved chats, models, memories) |
| `ChatViewTemplate` | The whole view: transcript, model card, status, toolbar, message box and buttons |
| `ChatUserMessageTemplate`, `ChatReplyTemplate`, `ChatNoticeTemplate` | DataTemplates for a `ChatMessageItem`: the user's messages, the replies, notices (or set `ChatView.MessageTemplateSelector`) |
| `ModelLoadCardTemplate` | The card that downloads and loads the model (`ModelLoadCard`) |
| `ReplyViewTemplate`, `ReplyParagraphStyle`, `ReplyMathStyle` | A reply's text (`ReplyView`: Markdown, links, maths) |
| `CodeBlockViewTemplate` | A code block (`CodeBlockView`) |
| `ThinkingViewTemplate` | The thinking: "Thinking...", then "Thought for N s", tapped to show it (`ThinkingView`) |
| `ChatAttachmentViewTemplate` | An image or audio attachment (`ChatAttachmentView`) |
| `ChatSendButtonStyle`, `ChatStopButtonStyle`, `ChatAttachButtonStyle`, `ChatDecorationTextStyle` | Styles of the buttons and of text decorations |
| `ChatTalkButtonStyle`, `ChatTalkStopButtonStyle` | The microphone, and the microphone while recording |

A control template names its working parts `PART_...`; a template without one of them just doesn't have that feature (e.g. no `PART_Attach`, no attach button). `ChatViewTemplate`'s parts are `PART_Scroll` (a ScrollView), `PART_Transcript` (the Layout the messages go in), `PART_Empty`, `PART_LoadCard` (a `ModelLoadCard`), `PART_Status` (a Label), `PART_Toolbar` and `PART_Header` (ContentViews), `PART_Input` (an InputView, e.g. an Editor), `PART_Send`, `PART_Stop`, `PART_Attach`, `PART_Talk` (Buttons, or views that are tapped) and `PART_Attachments` (a Layout for attachments waiting to be sent). The other controls list theirs in their documentation; `Themes/ChatStyles.xaml` in the source repository is a complete example.

Message templates bind to `ChatMessageItem` (`Text`, `Thinking`, `Steps`, `Attachments`, `Transcript`, `Decorations`, `IsStreaming`, `IsInterrupted`, `IsFailed`, `StartsContext`...), and can use the package's `ReplyView`, `ThinkingView`, `ChatAttachmentView` and `ChatDecorationView` (which shows a decoration: a view as it is, anything else as text). A long saved chat is shown a few messages at a time, the latest first, so opening it doesn't hold the UI thread (`IsRenderingTranscript`).

